  "items": [
    {
      "type": "post",
      "id": "7457361306031960064",
      "entityId": "7457361306031960064",
      "linkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/stuart-hardman_we-want-a-new-system-but-can-it-still-do-activity-7457361306031960064-Zc2O",
      "shareLinkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:share:7457361304169783296",
      "shareUrn": "urn:li:share:7457361304169783296",
      "content": "“We want a new system… but can it still do what the old one did?”\n\n20+ years and I still hear this in almost every project.\n\nIt starts full of ambition:\n“We want something modern, streamlined, fully integrated”\n\nThen about 10 minutes later:\n“Can it create that report we print every Monday”\n“Can we keep that workaround?”\n“Can it look the same?”\n\nAt this point I’m usually thinking… so you want a new system, but as a tribute act to the old one. 🤔 \n\nAnd to be fair, it’s understandable. It’s human.\n\nPeople don’t ask for the old system because they loved it. They ask for it because they understood it.\n\nThat slightly awkward process\nThat Excel report nobody trusts but everyone uses\nThat workaround no one admits owning\n\n…they’re familiar. And familiar wins, every time.\n\nThe reality is:\nNew system projects aren’t really about replacing software.\nThey’re about replacing habits.\nAnd that’s the bit that’s hardest to price, plan, or document.\n\nBecause software is logical. People… less so.\n\nMy default response is to go back to what my kids taught me years ago - \"Why?\" once you ask it a few times you can normally spot the reasoning behind it. Then you can start to address it!\n\nCurious how others handle this balance…\n\n👉 How far do you go to keep things familiar versus actually moving forward?",
